<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tulu Nadu</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tulu Nadu is the region of south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> where the Tulu language is spoken, comprising the Dakshina <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kannada">Kannada</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/udupi">Udupi</a> districts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnataka">Karnataka</a> and part of Kasaragod in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerala">Kerala</a>. It was ruled by the Alupa dynasty for much of the first and early second millennia CE and later absorbed into Vijayanagara. It has distinctive traditions such as Bhuta Kola spirit worship and Yakshagana <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tulu-nadu.jpg" alt="Tulu Nadu" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mr anonymousMr · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tulunadu_Region.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Tulu Nadu lies on the Konkan Coast between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabian-sea">Arabian Sea</a> to the west and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-ghats">Western Ghats</a> to the east, and covers about 8,441 square kilometres. Historically it stretched from the Gangavalli River in the north to Nileshwar, with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chandragiri">Chandragiri</a> river as a traditional southern border. The climate is tropical: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monsoon">monsoon</a> begins in June, the heaviest rain falls in the month of Aati, from mid-July to mid-August, and winter lasts from December to early February. The name appears as Tuḷu Nāṭṭu in the Akananuru, one of the Eight Anthologies of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sangam-literature">Sangam literature</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sangam-literature">Sangam literature</a> counts Tulu Nadu among the twelve regions of ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tamilakam">Tamilakam</a>, and it probably formed part of the Chera realm until it separated sometime between 300 and 500 CE, when the Kadambas invaded the northern Chera lands. The Alupas, its oldest known native dynasty, were feudatories of the Kadambas of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banavasi">Banavasi</a>, then of the Rashtrakutas, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chalukya-dynasty">Chalukyas</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hoysala-empire">Hoysalas</a> and Vijayanagara, though their subordination was largely nominal. The Greco-Roman geographer Ptolemy's Olokhoira is widely taken to be a corruption of Alva Kheda, the land of the Alvas.</p><p>Under Vijayanagara the region was governed as Mangaluru Rajya and Barkuru Rajya and grew very prosperous, with local houses such as the Chowtas of Ullal and Moodabidri and the Ajilas of Venur ruling as feudatories. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/keladi-nayakas">Keladi Nayakas</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ikkeri">Ikkeri</a> followed. Konkanis came by sea from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goa">Goa</a>, and Catholics in large numbers in the 16th century. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyder-ali">Hyder Ali</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mysore">Mysore</a> conquered the region in the mid- to late 18th century, and after the British defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tipu-sultan">Tipu Sultan</a> in 1799 it became the district of South Canara in the Madras Presidency. In the linguistic reorganisation of 1956 it passed to Mysore state, later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnataka">Karnataka</a>, while Kasaragod went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerala">Kerala</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mangalore">Mangaluru</a>, or Kudla, is the largest city, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/udupi">Udupi</a> and Kasaragod the other main towns. Tulu, a Dravidian language once written in the Tulu–Tigalari script derived from Grantha, had about 1.7 million speakers in 2001, and many communities follow the matrilineal Aliyasantana system of inheritance. The Jains left temples at Moodabidri and monolithic statues of Bahubali at Karkala, Venoor and Dharmasthala. After 1956 Tuluvas began to demand official status for Tulu and a state of their own, a cause taken up by groups such as the Tulu Rajya Horata Samiti.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tulu Nadu” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>From the 14th century the Alupas came under the Vijayanagara Empire, and their line ends in the records around 1444, when <b>Tulu Nadu</b> was absorbed into Vijayanagara administration.</blockquote><a href="/state/alupa-dynasty">Alupa dynasty</a></li></ul></section>
